ah, the classic "am I going insane or is React?" question. you're not going insane. it's React.

in development mode, if you have `<React.StrictMode>` wrapping your app (which is the default in create-react-app and other starters), React deliberately mounts your component, unmounts it, and then remounts it immediately. it's basically setting a small, controlled fire in your house to make sure your smoke alarm works.

the point is to shake out any bugs in your `useEffect`'s cleanup function. if you're setting up a subscription, a timer, or an event listener, you need to tear it down perfectly in the `return` function of your effect. if you don't, you'll get memory leaks and all sorts of weird shit in a real app. this double-invoke is React's way of forcing you to write clean-up code that actually cleans up.

so if your effect looks like this:

```javascript
useEffect(() => {
 // side effect happens here
 console.log('component mounted');

 return () => {
 // cleanup happens here
 console.log('component will unmount');
 };
}, []);
```

you'll see "component mounted", then "component will unmount", then "component mounted" again in your console. it's annoying as fuck, but it's a feature, not a bug.

a senior engineer I used to work with had a line for it: "React breaks your code in development so your users don't break your company in production." he was a prick, but he wasn't wrong.

it won't happen in the production build. you can either write a proper cleanup function, or if it's truly driving you mad, you can find `<React.StrictMode>` in your `index.js` and comment it out. I'd recommend the former. what's the effect actually trying to do?

