ah, the silent assassin of javascript. nine times out of ten, probably ninety-nine times out of a hundred, this is an `async` problem.

your `try` block is a synchronous bouncer. it runs, checks for trouble, and then immediately goes home. the error, meanwhile, is from an asynchronous party guest — a promise, a `setTimeout`, a network request — who shows up three hours late and starts throwing chairs long after your bouncer has clocked off. the `try-catch` is simply not around anymore to see the carnage.

if you have something like this:

```javascript
try {
 // this function returns a promise that will reject later
 someAsyncOperation(); 
} catch (e) {
 // this will never, ever run
 console.log("caught it!", e);
}
```

...it's never going to work. the `try` block *successfully* starts the operation and then finishes. job done. the error happens later, in another turn of the event loop, unobserved and alone.

the fix is to make the bouncer wait. you either use `await` inside the `try` block, which pauses the function until the promise settles:

```javascript
try {
 await someAsyncOperation();
} catch (e) {
 // this WILL run
 console.log("got him.", e);
}
```

or you chain a `.catch()` directly onto the promise itself, which is the old-school way of doing it.

as my first senior dev used to say, with the weary expression of a man who'd seen too many race conditions, "99% of bugs are just time travel gone wrong." your `catch` block is in the present; your error is in the future.

