you're not wrong. on a bad day, they absolutely feel like callbacks with a bit of bureaucratic scaffolding. but the extra steps are the entire point — they exist to fix the profound, soul-destroying mess that callbacks make at any kind of scale.

the original sin of callbacks is what nerds call "inversion of control." you give your function (the callback) to some other function and just... hope. you hope it gets called. you hope it gets called only once. you hope it doesn't just swallow your errors and die silently in a server log you'll never read. this is how you end up with the "pyramid of doom," a rightward-drifting nightmare of nested brackets that looks like a geological formation.

a promise, on the other hand, is an object. it's a receipt. an IOU from the JavaScript runtime saying "I owe you one value, to be delivered later." you get this receipt *immediately*, and you can decide what to do with it. you can tack on a `.then()` to say what happens on success. you can tack on a `.catch()` to handle any fuckups that happen *anywhere* in the chain, instead of writing `if (err)` in every single goddamn callback.

it turns this:

```javascript
doSomething(function(result) {
 doSomethingElse(result, function(newResult) {
 doThirdThing(newResult, function(finalResult) {
 console.log('finally, the thing: ' + finalResult);
 }, failureCallback);
 }, failureCallback);
}, failureCallback);
```

into this:

```javascript
doSomething()
 .then(result => doSomethingElse(result))
 .then(newResult => doThirdThing(newResult))
 .then(finalResult => {
 console.log('finally, the thing: ' + finalResult);
 })
 .catch(failureCallback);
```

a friend who teaches this stuff has a great line: "callbacks are like asking a favour; promises are like signing a contract." one is based on hope, the other on a guarantee you can actually inspect and chain things onto. and it's all just the groundwork for `async/await`, which is promises dressed up in even prettier, more readable clothes.

so yeah, extra steps. but they're the steps that take you from a shaky, hope-based system to something that actually resembles a sane, readable program.

ah, the ancient CSS ritual of 'why the fuck is there a white bar on the edge of my screen.' it's a rite of passage.

usually it's one of two culprits: either the element you've put the background on isn't actually filling the screen, or you haven't told the browser *how* to scale the image. the browser, by default, is a very stupid and very literal child who will do exactly what you say, not what you mean.

try this on your `body` or whatever container you're using:

```css
body {
 /* make sure the body is actually the full height of the viewport */
 min-height: 100vh;

 /* your image here, obviously */
 background-image: url('path/to/your/epic/background.jpg');

 /* now the magic bit */
 background-size: cover;
 background-position: center;
 background-repeat: no-repeat;
}
```

the key is `background-size: cover`. it tells the browser to scale the image up until it fills the entire container, even if it has to chop off the edges a bit to maintain the aspect ratio. think of it like stretching a fitted sheet over a mattress. `background-position: center` just makes sure it crops from the edges, not from the middle where the interesting stuff usually is.

