ah, welcome to the club. you've run into javascript's favourite little prank, the one it plays on everyone. my guess is you're dealing with a classic pass-by-reference-not-by-value situation.

in javascript, when you're dealing with simple things like numbers or strings, assigning a variable to another one makes a fresh copy. simple. clean. sane.

objects, however, are little bastards. when you do `let newObject = oldObject`, you're not making a copy of the object. you're making a copy of the *address* of the object in memory. think of it like this: you're not getting a photocopy of my house's floor plan, you're getting a second key to my front door. if you go in and paint the living room purple, I'm going to come home and find a purple living room.

so when you change a property on `newObject`, you're also changing it on `oldObject`, because they're both pointing at the exact same chunk of data.

here's the crime scene:

```javascript
let person = { name: 'monty', age: 30 };

// you think you're making a copy...
let samePerson = person;

// ...but you're just getting a new key to the same house.
samePerson.age = 31;

// now look what you've done.
console.log(person.age); // outputs 31. shit.
```

the fix is to make an actual, shallow copy. the modern, non-headache way is with the spread syntax (`...`):

```javascript
let person = { name: 'monty', age: 30 };

// this time, we're building a new house from the old floor plan
let newPerson = { ...person };

// now we're just painting our *new* house
newPerson.age = 31;

console.log(person.age); // outputs 30. hooray.
console.log(newPerson.age); // outputs 31. as intended.
```

this is why frameworks like React get so pissy about "mutating state directly." they rely on seeing a brand new object to know that something has actually changed and they need to re-render the screen. if you just change a property on the old object, React just shrugs, because as far as it's concerned, it's the same object it saw last time.

a senior engineer I used to work with always said, "the most dangerous line in javascript is `let a = b` when you don't know for sure what `b` is." he was right more often than he was wrong.

